\DOC LOCAL_SQR_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_SQR_TAC : int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Discharges one call to a modular-squaring subroutine inside an {eventually}
machine-execution goal, advancing the symbolic state to a fresh successor state
and restating the state-dependent assumptions for it, including a conditional
specification of the squared result written to the destination buffer.

\DESCRIBE
{LOCAL_SQR_TAC} takes one {int} argument (displayed as a small numeral): the
index of the new symbolic state it produces, so that the goal's state variable
and the rewritten assumptions are all phrased in terms of state number n+...
corresponding to that index. The {eventually} postcondition of the goal is
preserved, but its final state argument becomes the newly indexed state. Every
assumption mentioning the previous state — program-counter, stack-pointer,
register, flag, memory-content, {bytes_loaded} and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE}
relation — is removed and re-added in the new state, with the following
differences: flag assumptions and the subroutine's argument-pointer register
assumptions are dropped rather than carried over; the program counter and stack
pointer are updated to the return address and caller stack frame; the
{MAYCHANGE} relation is extended with the registers and memory regions
clobbered by the call; and a new implicational assumption is added stating
that, under a bound on the input, the destination buffer holds the Montgomery
square of the input modulo the field prime. Purely mathematical and
non-overlapping assumptions are left untouched.
{
   A ?- eventually ... (previous state)
   ================================================  LOCAL_SQR_TAC n
   A' ?- eventually ... (state n+...)
}
\noindent where {A'} is {A} with the state-dependent assumptions replaced as
described and the new implicational assumption added.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces only this single kind of transformation occurs, on goals
of this {eventually} machine-execution form.

\ENDDOC
